        "text": "Learning the causal structure from purely observational data (i.e., causal discovery) is a fundamental but daunting task (Chickering et al., 2004; Shen et al., 2020). It strives to identify causal relationships between variables and encodes the conditional independencies as a directed acyclic graph (DAG). Differentiable score-based optimization is a crucial enabler of causal discovery (Vowels et al., 2021). Specifically, it is formulated as a continuous constraint optimization problem by minimizing the average score function and a smooth acyclicity constraint. To guarantee the structure is fully or partially identifiable (see Section 2), researchers impose stringent restrictions on model parametric family (e.g., linear, additive) and common assumptions of variable distributions (e.g., homogeneity data) (Peters et al., 2014; Ng et al., 2019a). Following this scheme, recent follow-on studies (Kalainathan et al., 2018; Ng et al., 2019b; Zhu et al., 2020; Khemakhem et al., 2021; Yu et al., 2021) extend the formulation to tackle general nonlinear problems by utilizing a variety of deep learning models.\nHowever, upon careful inspections, we spot and justify two unsatisfactory behaviors of the current differentiable score-based methods:\n\u2022 The score-based causal discovery is error-prone to learning spurious edges or reverse causal directions between variables, which derails the structure learning accuracy (He et al., 2021; Ng et al., 2022). We substantiate our claim with illustrative examples as shown in Figure 1. We find that even the fundamental structure, i.e., chain, in a linear system, is easily misidentified by the state-of-the-art method, NOTEARS (Zheng et al., 2018).\n\u2022 Despite being appealing in synthetic data, differentiable score-based causal discovery methods suffer from severe performance degradation when encountering heterogeneous data (Huang et al., 2020; 2019). Considering Figure 1, NOTEARS is susceptible to learning redundant causations when the distributions of noise variables vary.\nTaking a closer look at this dominant score-based causal discovery scheme, i.e., optimizing the DAG learner via an average score function under strict assumptions, we ascribe these undesirable behaviors to its inherent limitations:\n\u2022 The collecting datasets naturally include an overwhelming number of easy samples and a small number of informative samples that might contain crucial causation information (Shrivastava et al., 2016). Averagely scoring the observational samples deprives the discovery process of differentiating sample importance, thus easy samples dominate the learning of DAG. As a result, prevailing score-based techniques fail to learn true causal relationship but instead exploit the easier-to-fit spurious edges.\n\u2022 Noise distribution shifts are inevitable and common in real-world training, as the observations are typically collected at different periods, environments, locations, and so forth (Arjovsky et al., 2019). As a result, the strong assumption of noise homogeneity for differentiable DAG learner is easily violated in real-world data (Peters et al., 2016). A line of works (Ghassami et al., 2018; Wang et al., 2022) dedicated to heterogeneous data can successfully address this issue. However, they often require explicit domain annotations (i.e., ideal partition according to heterogeneity underlying the data) for each sample, which are prohibitively expensive and hard to obtain (Creager et al., 2021), further limiting their applicability.\nTo reshape the optimization scheme and resolve these limitations, we propose to adaptively reweight the samples, which de facto concurrently mitigates the influence of spurious edges and generalizes to heterogeneous data. The core idea is to discover and upweight a set of less-fitted samples that offer additional insight to depict the causal edges, compared to the samples that can be easily fitted via the spurious edges. Focusing more on less-fitted samples enables the DAG learner to effectively generalize to heterogeneous data since in real-world scenarios, such samples typically come from disadvantaged domains. Due to the difficulty of accessing domain annotation, distinguishing such disadvantaged but informative samples and automatically assigning adaptive weights is challenging.\nWith the idea of adaptive sample reweighting, we present a simple yet effective model-agnostic optimization framework for DAG learner, coined ReScore, which alleviates the overfitting issue of easier-to-fit spurious edges. On the other hand, ReScore tackles heterogeneous data without knowing the domain annotation in advance by automatically learning the instance-wise adaptive weights. More precisely, we reformulate the adaptive weights learning as a bilevel optimization problem. The general model-agnostic bilevel scheme is designed below:\n\u2022 In the inner loop, the DAG learner is evaluated by the reweighted score function to quantify the reliance on easier-to-fit samples and optimize the adaptive weights to achieve the worst-case.\n\u2022 In the outer loop, any score-based causal discovery method can be deployed to obtain the optimal DAG learner on weighted observational data where the weights are determined by the inner loop.\nThe inner loop and outer loop are alternatively trained. Benefiting from this optimization scheme, our ReScore has three desirable properties. First, ReScore is a model-agnostic technique that is flexible enough to easily adapt to any score-based causal discovery method. Moreover, we theoretically reveal that the structure identifiability is inherited by ReScore from the original causal discovery method (cf. Theorem 1). Second, ReScore jointly lightens the negative effect of spurious edge learning and performance drop in heterogeneous data by auto-learnable adaptive weights. Theoretical analysis of adaptive properties for weights is provided in Section 3.3 (cf. Theorem 2). Third, our proposed ReScore s